<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      ONE PERMISSION, and only because a measured capability needs it.

      INTERNET - REQUIRED by HttpInferenceBackend, which POSTs to the on-device llama-server at
                 the literal http://127.0.0.1:8080. Android gates AF_INET sockets on the AID_INET
                 group, which is granted by this permission, so loopback is NOT exempt: without it
                 the connection fails with EACCES. Verified on this device, not assumed.

                 CONTAINMENT: the endpoint is a private constant in HttpInferenceBackend, never a
                 job field, so a job document cannot point this app at any other host. The only
                 reachable peer is the local server the project already runs and trusts.

                 INTERNET alone is NOT sufficient, and this was also discovered by real-device
                 failure: the first run died with "Cleartext HTTP traffic to 127.0.0.1 not
                 permitted", because Android blocks cleartext by default since targetSdk 28 and
                 does not exempt loopback. res/xml/network_security_config.xml therefore permits
                 cleartext for 127.0.0.1 and localhost ONLY, with everything else still denied.

                 This is a deliberate, reversible departure from the earlier "zero permissions"
                 posture (D5). It is recorded here rather than in a commit message because the
                 manifest is where the next reader looks. Removing the single line below disables
                 the http backend and restores the previous posture; the default backend
                 (llama-jni) never opens a socket at all.

      Still explicitly NOT declared:
        POST_NOTIFICATIONS          - there is no Service and no notification
        FOREGROUND_SERVICE          - there is no Service
        READ/WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE - not needed for the app's OWN external directory
        MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE     - would grant whole-storage access; unnecessary and dangerous
        WAKE_LOCK                   - the workload is bounded and runs while the Activity is up
        REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES    - the worker installs nothing

      No <service>, no <receiver>, no <provider>. One Activity.
    -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar"
        android:label="PixelDesktop DTCA-1 Worker"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config">

        <!--
          exported="true" IS REQUIRED, and was discovered by real-device failure, not assumed.

          The first build used exported="false" on the assumption that adb (uid=2000 shell) holds a
          privileged permission to start non-exported components. It does not. Launching produced:

            java.lang.SecurityException: Permission Denial: starting Intent
            { cmp=com.pixeldesktop.worker/.WorkerActivity } from null (pid=..., uid=2000)
            not exported from uid 10004

          So the Activity must be exported for ADB to start it.

          CONTAINMENT, given that it is exported:
            - There is NO intent-filter. Only an explicit component start
              (`am start -n com.pixeldesktop.worker/.WorkerActivity`) can reach it. No other app
              can start it implicitly via MAIN/LAUNCHER.
            - The Activity accepts NO Intent extras. It takes no input from its caller.
            - WorkerActivity additionally verifies the launching UID at runtime and refuses to run
              for any launcher other than adb shell or root. See getLaunchedFromUid there.

          The residual exposure is therefore: another app may trigger a re-run of whatever job file
          is already present. It cannot supply input, read the result, or alter behaviour.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".WorkerActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:noHistory="true" />

    </application>

</manifest>
